  "body": "### What is the issue with the HTML Standard?\n\nIn the MDN article entitled “Using the Popover API,” there is a section devoted to accessibility issues, which includes the following statement: “_To allow AT such as screen readers to make sense of the relationship between the invoker and the popover, an implicit aria-details and aria-expanded relationship is set up between them_.”\n(See : https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Popover_API/Using#popover_accessibility_features.)\n\nBut in the spec, we just find: \"When using popover on elements without accessibility semantics, for instance the div element, authors should use the appropriate ARIA attributes to ensure the popover is accessible.\" \n(See: https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/popover.html#the-popover-attribute)\nIndeed, when I test (only with VoiceOver for now), no implicit semantics occur when interacting with the element displayed by popover.\n\nI posted an issue in September 2025 on MDN's GitHub, but it has remained unanswered (https://github.com/mdn/content/issues/37465). Do you have any insights? Are these behaviors coming in the future? Or is MDN wrong on this point?\n",
